Toward a Mathematical Theory of the Minimum Viable Ecosystem for Extraterrestrial Life Support

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : chatGpt, Grok XAi, Meta AI, Deepseek 


1. Introduction

Sustaining a human crew beyond Earth without continuous resupply demands an artificial ecosystem that regenerates atmosphere, purifies water, recycles waste, and produces food. Earth’s biosphere performs these functions through an intricate web of interacting species and abiotic processes, but no general theory exists for constructing the smallest such system tailored to a given planetary environment. Experimental closed‑ecological life‑support (CELSS) programmes—MELiSSA (Godia et al., 2002), BIOS‑3 (Gitelson et al., 2003), Biosphere 2 (Nelson et al., 1999)—have demonstrated partial closure and revealed critical failure modes, yet every design was empirical and tuned to terrestrial testbeds. There is no unified mathematical framework that, from first principles, determines the minimal set of biological guilds and physico‑chemical processors required for long‑term viability under arbitrary planetary boundary conditions.

This PhD project addresses that gap by formulating the Minimum Viable Ecosystem (MVE) problem and developing rigorous existence and persistence results. The framework rests on a hybrid‑stochastic‑dynamical‑system representation of an ecosystem and draws on stoichiometric network theory, Chemical Reaction Network Theory (CRNT), permanence theory for competitive Kolmogorov systems, and large‑deviation estimates for extinction probabilities. The ultimate aim is to produce a mathematically sound foundation for minimal life‑support design, applicable to the Moon, Mars, and orbital habitats. Crucially, the proposal distinguishes between well‑established mathematical building blocks and novel conjectures that will be proven during the PhD. All theorems presented are labelled as Proposed Theorems (Conjectures); their full proofs constitute the core original contributions of the research.

---

2. Research Objectives

1. Formalise an ecosystem as the tuple \mathcal{E} = (S, Q, R, I, E, C) and define its dynamics by a hybrid stochastic differential equation (SDE) with regime switching.
2. Propose and prove a Stoichiometric–Thermodynamic Feasibility Theorem (Conjecture 1) giving necessary and sufficient conditions for the existence of a strictly positive, thermodynamically feasible steady state. The proof will combine Farkas’ lemma, monotone rate‑law inversion, and a homotopy continuation from the closed, deficiency‑zero network to the open system.
3. Propose and prove a Uniform Persistence Theorem (Conjecture 2) for closed ecosystems governed by a smooth regularisation of Liebig’s minimum law, establishing an algebraic criterion for permanent coexistence. Extend this to stochastic permanence with an explicit extinction‑probability bound derived from the Freidlin–Wentzell quasipotential for hybrid diffusions.
4. Formulate the MVE as a mixed‑integer nonlinear program (MINLP) whose objective minimises the number of biological guilds and abiotic processors, subject to stoichiometric, thermodynamic, and persistence constraints.
5. Design a computational MVE‑Engine (in Julia) that implements these theorems to automatically generate provably minimal life‑support configurations; validate it against historical CELSS data and apply it to lunar and Martian parameter sets.

The novelty lies in the rigorous unification of these tools. While each component is individually well‑studied, combining them into a single deductive chain that yields a constructive MVE design is an open problem.

