3. Mathematical Formalisation of an Ecosystem

We work on a filtered probability space (\Omega,\mathcal{F},\{\mathcal{F}_t\},\mathbb{P}) supporting an m-dimensional Wiener process \mathbf{W} and an independent continuous‑time Markov chain \theta(t) on a finite state space \mathcal{M}=\{1,\dots,K\} with generator matrix Q=(q_{ij}).

Definition 3.1 (Ecosystem tuple).

\mathcal{E} = (S, Q, R, I, E, C),

where

· S = \{s_1,\dots,s_{n_s}\} – biological functional guilds, each represented by a biomass x_i(t) \ge 0 and an elemental stoichiometry vector \mathbf{s}_i \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}^{n_e} for the elements \{\text{C, H, O, N, P, S}\}.
· Q = \{q_1,\dots,q_{n_Q}\} – abiotic processors, each with a binary inclusion variable y_j \in \{0,1\} and a fixed power demand p_j. Their operation is encoded in the stoichiometric matrix \mathbf{N}(\mathbf{y}) and may be subject to energy‑budget constraints.
· R – vector of abiotic resource pools \mathbf{r}(t) \in \mathbb{R}_{+}^m (e.g., \mathrm{H_2O, O_2, CO_2}, mineral nutrients).
· I = (\mathbf{N}, \mathbf{f}) – interaction structure. \mathbf{N}(\mathbf{y}) \in \mathbb{R}^{(n_s+m) \times n_r} is the stoichiometric matrix of n_r internal processes, parametrised by the active processors \mathbf{y}. \mathbf{f}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{r}; \Pi) is the rate vector; \Pi = (\Pi_1,\dots,\Pi_{d_\Pi}) are dimensionless groups capturing the planetary environment, derived from Buckingham \Pi-theorem analysis.
· E – external energy flux, supplied as photosynthetically active radiation and appearing as a substrate in phototrophic rate laws.
· C – planetary constraints, i.e., fixed bounds on the \Pi-groups.

The dynamics are given by the hybrid SDE with regime switching:

d\begin{pmatrix} \mathbf{x} \\ \mathbf{r} \end{pmatrix}
= \mathbf{N}(\mathbf{y})\, \mathbf{f}_{\theta(t)}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{r}; \Pi)\, dt
+ \boldsymbol{\sigma}_{\theta(t)}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{r})\, d\mathbf{W}(t),
\tag{1}

with initial law supported on the “safe set” \Omega_0 = \{(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{r}) : x_i \ge \varepsilon_0,\, r_j \ge \rho_0 \,\forall i,j\}. The deterministic skeleton is obtained by fixing \theta to a nominal regime and setting \boldsymbol{\sigma} \equiv 0.

Growth kinetics. To avoid the mathematical difficulties arising from the non‑differentiability of Liebig’s minimum law, we adopt the regularised p-harmonic mean:

\mu_i^{(p)}(\mathbf{r}) = \Big( \sum_{j \in \mathcal{R}_i} \mu_{ij}(r_j)^{-p} \Big)^{-1/p}, \qquad
\mu_{ij}(r) = \frac{\mu_{\max,ij}\, r}{K_{ij} + r},
\tag{2}

where \mathcal{R}_i is the set of resources limiting guild i. The limit p \to \infty recovers the exact minimum law: \mu_i^{(\infty)}(\mathbf{r}) = \min_{j \in \mathcal{R}_i} \mu_{ij}(r_j). All theoretical proofs will first be established for finite, sufficiently large p, guaranteeing \mathcal{C}^\infty smoothness; the singular limit p\to\infty will be studied via convergence of attractors.

