4. Stoichiometric–Thermodynamic Feasibility (Conjecture 1)

Consider the deterministic skeleton with a fixed processor configuration \mathbf{y} and a single regime. The steady‑state condition is \mathbf{N}\,\mathbf{f}^* = \mathbf{b}, where \mathbf{b} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_s+m} collects all net external demands (crew harvest, leakage, concrete carbonation, etc.). Internal processes must satisfy the second law: for every active flux f_k^* > 0,

\Delta G_k(\mathbf{r}^*) < 0.
\tag{3}

Using the linearised thermodynamic potential \Delta G_k(\mathbf{r}) = \Delta G_k^\circ + RT\sum_{j} \nu_{kj} \ln r_j, condition (3) becomes a linear inequality in \mathbf{u} = \ln \mathbf{r}:

\boldsymbol{\nu}_k^{\!\top} \mathbf{u} < -\frac{\Delta G_k^\circ}{RT} =: c_k.
\tag{4}

Define the flux cone \mathcal{F}(\mathbf{y}) = \{ \mathbf{f} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_r}_+ : \mathbf{N}(\mathbf{y})\mathbf{f} = \mathbf{b} \} and the thermodynamic cone \mathcal{T} = \{ \mathbf{u} \in \mathbb{R}^m : \mathbf{A}\mathbf{u} < \mathbf{c} \}, where the rows of \mathbf{A} are the vectors \boldsymbol{\nu}_k^{\!\top} for all reactions with finite \Delta G_k^\circ.

Conjecture 1 (Feasible steady state).
Assume that all rate functions f_k(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{r}) are continuous, strictly positive on the interior, and strictly monotone in each resource argument, and that the closed subsystem (\mathbf{b}=0) is weakly reversible and has deficiency \delta = 0. Assume further that the open‑system Jacobian \mathbf{J}_{\text{open}}(\epsilon) = \mathbf{N}\frac{\partial \mathbf{f}}{\partial (\mathbf{x},\mathbf{r})} remains nonsingular along the homotopy path \mathbf{N}\mathbf{f} = \epsilon\mathbf{b},\; \epsilon\in[0,1]. Then a strictly positive steady state (\mathbf{x}^*,\mathbf{r}^*) > 0 with thermodynamically feasible fluxes exists if and only if

\begin{aligned}
\text{(i)}\quad & \mathbf{b} \in \operatorname{cone}(\mathbf{N}), \quad \text{i.e., } \exists \mathbf{f} \ge 0 \text{ such that } \mathbf{N}\mathbf{f} = \mathbf{b},\\[4pt]
\text{(ii)}\quad & \mathcal{T} \neq \emptyset \text{ and there exists } \mathbf{f} \in \mathcal{F} \text{ whose corresponding log‑resource vector lies in } \mathcal{T}.
\end{aligned}

Moreover, when it exists, the steady state is unique in its stoichiometric compatibility class and is locally asymptotically stable.

Planned proof strategy (to be completed during the PhD).
Necessity is immediate. For sufficiency, we will construct a homotopy from the closed deficiency‑zero network (\mathbf{b}=0) to the open system (\epsilon=1). For \epsilon=0 the Deficiency Zero Theorem guarantees a unique positive equilibrium. Using the nonsingularity assumption and the Implicit Function Theorem, we will extend this branch to \epsilon=1 provided the path does not cross a bifurcation. To treat larger \mathbf{b} for which the homotopy cannot be directly applied, we will develop a fixed‑point argument on a compact, convex subset of the log‑resource space \mathcal{T} intersected with the projection of \mathcal{F} via the inverse rate mapping \mathbf{u} \mapsto \mathbf{x} = \operatorname{diag}(\mu^{(p)}(\mathbf{r}))^{-1}\mathbf{f}. Strict monotonicity of the regularised growth laws and the linearity of the thermodynamic constraints ensure the existence of a fixed point by Brouwer’s theorem; uniqueness and stability then follow from the properties of the underlying weakly reversible network perturbed by a P‑matrix structured Jacobian. Complete rigour demands a careful verification of the compactness of the feasible set and the continuity of the constructed map, which will be a major part of the first year’s work.

Remark 1 (Biosphere 2). In Biosphere 2, unaccounted \mathrm{CO_2} sequestration by concrete carbonation violated condition (i) because the external sink vector was not in the cone of the nominal stoichiometric matrix. Conjecture 1 would thus correctly predict the impossibility of a steady state, rationalising the observed \mathrm{O_2} decline.

