5. Persistence and Stochastic Permanence (Conjecture 2)

5.1 Deterministic persistence under regularised Liebig kinetics

We study the closed ecosystem (\mathbf{b}=0) with the smooth kinetics (2) for a fixed large p. The deterministic skeleton is

\begin{aligned}
\dot{x}_i &= x_i\big( \mu_i^{(p)}(\mathbf{r}) - D_i \big), \quad i=1,\dots,n_s,\\
\dot{r}_j &= \text{recycling}_j(\mathbf{x}) - \sum_{i: j \in \mathcal{R}_i} c_{ji}\, x_i\, \mu_i^{(p)}(\mathbf{r}),
\end{aligned}
\tag{5}

with mortality/dilution rates D_i and stoichiometric coefficients c_{ji}. The system is dissipative, keeping total mass bounded.

Conjecture 2 (Deterministic persistence).
Assume a strictly positive steady state \mathbf{z}^* > 0 exists (Conjecture 1). For any boundary equilibrium E_{\mathcal{J}} where the subset \mathcal{J} \subsetneq \{1,\dots,n_s\} is extinct, define the invasion growth rate of a missing guild i \notin \mathcal{J} as

\lambda_i(E_{\mathcal{J}}) = \mu_i^{(p)}(\mathbf{r}^*(E_{\mathcal{J}})) - D_i .
\tag{6}

The system is uniformly persistent if and only if for every boundary equilibrium E_{\mathcal{J}} there exists i \notin \mathcal{J} with \lambda_i(E_{\mathcal{J}}) > 0. In that case, there exists \kappa > 0 such that \liminf_{t\to\infty} x_i(t) \ge \kappa for all i, uniformly for initial conditions in the interior.

Proof outline. The regularised kinetics (2) render the vector field \mathcal{C}^\infty and competitive (i.e., \partial \dot{x}_i / \partial x_j \le 0 for j \neq i). Hence the system possesses a globally attracting carrying simplex \Sigma (Hirsch 1988; Smith 1995). Uniform persistence is equivalent to the boundary of \Sigma being a repellor. We will construct an average Lyapunov function P(\mathbf{x}) = \prod_i x_i^{p_i} with weights p_i>0. The time derivative \dot{P}/P = \sum_i p_i (\mu_i^{(p)} - D_i). By choosing p_i appropriately (solving a linear programming problem based on the invasion rates at all boundary equilibria), we can ensure that for every ergodic measure supported on the boundary, the average of \dot{P}/P is strictly positive. Since for competitive systems the boundary limit sets are equilibria, condition (6) is both necessary and sufficient. The uniform bound \kappa then follows from compactness of \Sigma and the strict positivity of the invasion rates. A key task of the PhD will be to prove that the linear programming problem has a feasible solution under the resource‑competition structure induced by Liebig–Monod trade‑offs.

Remark 2. In the exact minimum‑law limit (p\to\infty) the carrying simplex theory must be revisited because the vector field is only piecewise \mathcal{C}^1. We will investigate whether the persistence result carries over by approximating the min law with the smooth p-norm and letting p\to\infty; if necessary, non‑smooth persistence theorems (e.g., using Filippov solutions) will be adapted.

5.2 Stochastic permanence and extinction bounds

Return to the full hybrid SDE (1). Assume the noise intensity is small, \|\boldsymbol{\sigma}_\theta\| \le \sigma_0, and that \theta(t) is ergodic with stationary distribution \pi.

Conjecture 2′ (Stochastic permanence).
Under the deterministic persistence condition of Conjecture 2, and if the averaged invasion growth rates satisfy

\sum_{k=1}^K \pi_k \, \lambda_i^{(k)}(E_{\mathcal{J}}) > 0 \quad \text{for some } i \notin \mathcal{J},
\tag{7}

then for sufficiently small \sigma_0 the process \mathbf{Z}(t) = (\mathbf{x}(t),\mathbf{r}(t)) is stochastically permanent: there exists a unique stationary distribution supported on the interior, and the process is positive recurrent. Moreover, the extinction probability within a mission time T satisfies

\mathbb{P}\big( \min_{i} \inf_{[0,T]} x_i(t) < \varepsilon \big) \le C(T)\, \exp\!\Big( -\frac{I_\varepsilon}{\sigma_0^2} \Big),
\tag{8}

where I_\varepsilon is the hybrid quasipotential barrier from the interior attractor to the boundary layer \{x_i \le \varepsilon\}, defined as

V(\mathbf{z}, k) = \inf_{T, \boldsymbol{\varphi}, \theta} \Big\{ \frac12 \int_0^T \big\| \boldsymbol{\sigma}_{\theta(t)}^{-1}(\dot{\boldsymbol{\varphi}} - \mathbf{f}_{\theta}) \big\|^2 dt + \sum_{m} \ln\big( \tfrac{q_{\theta(t_m^-)\theta(t_m^+)}}{-q_{\theta(t_m^-)\theta(t_m^-)}} \big) \Big\}.
\tag{9}

The proof will adapt the Freidlin–Wentzell large‑deviation theory for hybrid diffusions (Budhiraja & Dupuis, 2000). The Lyapunov function V(\mathbf{x}) = -\sum_i p_i \ln x_i will be analysed near the boundary; the challenge is to show that the drift term dominates the diffusion term when the deterministic invasion condition holds, despite the singularity of the Hessian. A careful small‑noise analysis using the boundedness of \mu_i and the structure of \boldsymbol{\sigma} (which must vanish at the boundary or be dominated by the drift) will be undertaken.

