6. The MVE as an Optimisation Problem

The MVE for a given planetary environment is defined as the Pareto‑minimal set with respect to the number of biological guilds and abiotic processors. We formulate the following MINLP:

\begin{aligned}
\min_{\mathbf{u},\, \mathbf{y},\, \mathbf{x}^*,\, \mathbf{f}^*} \quad & \sum_{i=1}^{n_s} u_i + \sum_{j=1}^{n_Q} y_j \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & \mathbf{N}(\mathbf{y})\,\mathbf{f}^* = \mathbf{b}, \\
& \mathbf{A} \ln \mathbf{r}^* < \mathbf{c}, \\
& \mathbf{x}^* = \big(\operatorname{diag}(\boldsymbol{\mu}^{(p)}(\mathbf{r}^*))\big)^{-1} \mathbf{f}^*_{\text{guilds}}, \\
& \text{persistence cuts (Conjecture 2)}, \\
& u_i \in \{0,1\},\; y_j \in \{0,1\},\; \mathbf{x}^* > 0,\; \mathbf{r}^* > 0.
\end{aligned}

Bilinear terms \mathbf{N}(\mathbf{y})\mathbf{f}^* and x_i \mu_i^{(p)} will be handled by outer approximation and spatial branch‑and‑bound, not relying solely on big‑M. Persistence constraints will be enforced via lazy cuts: when an integer candidate (\mathbf{u},\mathbf{y}) is found, a sub‑solver (e.g., homotopy continuation) computes all boundary equilibria of the corresponding deterministic system. If an equilibrium lacks an invader with \lambda_i > 0, a “no‑good” cut such as \sum_{i \notin \mathcal{J}} u_i \ge 1 is added. Since the catalogue of candidate guilds is modest (10–20), the MINLP is computationally tractable.

