A.2 Dynamical Systems and Persistence

Consider an autonomous ordinary differential equation

\dot{\mathbf{z}} = \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{z}), \qquad \mathbf{z} \in \Omega \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n_+,

with \Omega positively invariant.

Definition A.1 (Uniform persistence).
Let \mathcal{I} \subseteq \{1,\dots,n\} be a set of essential components. The system is uniformly persistent with respect to \mathcal{I} if there exists \eta > 0 such that for all initial conditions with z_i(0) > 0 for i \in \mathcal{I},

\liminf_{t\to\infty} z_i(t) \ge \eta, \qquad \forall i \in \mathcal{I}.

Theorem A.2 (Average Lyapunov function; Hofbauer 1981).
Suppose \Omega is compact, forward invariant, and \mathbf{F} \in \mathcal{C}^1. If there exists a \mathcal{C}^1 function V : \operatorname{int} \Omega \to \mathbb{R} that extends continuously to \Omega with V(\mathbf{z}) \to \infty as \mathbf{z} \to \partial \Omega, and a continuous function \psi : \Omega \to \mathbb{R} such that

\dot{V}(\mathbf{z}) \ge \psi(\mathbf{z}) V(\mathbf{z}) \quad \text{on } \operatorname{int} \Omega,

and for every \omega-limit point \mathbf{z}^* of an interior trajectory one has

\limsup_{t\to\infty} \frac{1}{t} \int_0^t \psi(\mathbf{z}(s)) \, ds > 0,

then the system is uniformly persistent. A typical choice is V(\mathbf{z}) = -\sum_{i \in \mathcal{I}} \ln z_i, giving \dot{V}/V = -\sum_{i\in\mathcal{I}} \dot{z}_i/z_i.

Carrying simplex.
For competitive Kolmogorov systems of the form

\dot{x}_i = x_i f_i(\mathbf{x}), \qquad \frac{\partial f_i}{\partial x_j} \le 0 \quad (j \neq i),

there exists a globally attracting invariant hypersurface \Sigma of codimension one—the carrying simplex—that contains all non‑trivial asymptotic dynamics (Hirsch 1988; Smith 1995). Uniform persistence is equivalent to the boundary of \Sigma being a repellor.

A.3 Regularised Liebig–Monod Kinetics

For guild i with limiting resource set \mathcal{R}_i, we use the smooth p-harmonic mean (p \ge 1):

\mu_i^{(p)}(\mathbf{r}) = \bigg( \sum_{j \in \mathcal{R}_i} \mu_{ij}(r_j)^{-p} \bigg)^{-1/p}, \qquad
\mu_{ij}(r) = \frac{\mu_{\max,ij}\, r}{K_{ij} + r}.
\tag{A.1}

For p=1 this is the harmonic mean; as p \to \infty,

\lim_{p\to\infty} \mu_i^{(p)}(\mathbf{r}) = \min_{j \in \mathcal{R}_i} \mu_{ij}(r_j),

recovering Liebig’s law. The function \mu_i^{(p)} is \mathcal{C}^\infty on \mathbb{R}_{>0}^m, strictly positive, and strictly monotone in each r_j (j \in \mathcal{R}_i). The break‑even concentration for resource j at dilution rate D_i is

R_{ij}^* = \frac{K_{ij} D_i}{\mu_{\max,ij} - D_i},

provided \mu_{\max,ij} > D_i.

A.4 Chemical Reaction Network Theory (CRNT)

A reaction network \mathcal{N} = (\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{R}) consists of a set of complexes \mathcal{C} and reactions \mathcal{R}. The stoichiometric matrix \mathbf{N} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times r} maps reaction fluxes to species changes. The deficiency is

\delta = |\mathcal{C}| - \ell - s,

where \ell is the number of linkage classes and s = \operatorname{rank}(\mathbf{N}). A network is weakly reversible if every reaction is part of a directed cycle.

Theorem A.3 (Deficiency Zero Theorem; Feinberg 1987).
If a mass‑action network is weakly reversible and has deficiency zero, then each stoichiometric compatibility class \mathcal{P} = (\mathbf{c} + \operatorname{Im}\mathbf{N}) \cap \mathbb{R}_{>0}^n contains precisely one positive equilibrium, which is complex‑balanced and locally asymptotically stable.

A.5 Convex Cones and Farkas’ Lemma

For a matrix \mathbf{A} \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times n},

\operatorname{cone}(\mathbf{A}) = \{ \mathbf{A}\mathbf{x} : \mathbf{x} \ge 0 \}.

Farkas’ lemma. \mathbf{b} \in \operatorname{cone}(\mathbf{A}) if and only if for every \mathbf{y} \in \mathbb{R}^m with \mathbf{y}^{\!\top}\mathbf{A} \ge 0, one has \mathbf{y}^{\!\top}\mathbf{b} \ge 0.

A.6 Thermodynamic Linearisation

For reaction k with net consumption vector \boldsymbol{\nu}_k (negative for products), the Gibbs free energy change is

\Delta G_k(\mathbf{r}) = \Delta G_k^\circ + RT \sum_j \nu_{kj} \ln r_j.

The condition \Delta G_k < 0 becomes, with \mathbf{u} = \ln \mathbf{r},

\boldsymbol{\nu}_k^{\!\top} \mathbf{u} < -\frac{\Delta G_k^\circ}{RT} =: c_k.

The thermodynamic cone is the open convex polyhedron

\mathcal{T} = \{ \mathbf{u} \in \mathbb{R}^m : \mathbf{A}\mathbf{u} < \mathbf{c} \},

where the rows of \mathbf{A} are the \boldsymbol{\nu}_k^{\!\top} for all reactions with finite \Delta G_k^\circ.

A.7 Stochastic Differential Equations with Regime Switching

On a filtered probability space (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \{\mathcal{F}_t\}, \mathbb{P}), let \mathbf{W} be an m-dimensional Wiener process and \theta(t) a continuous‑time Markov chain on \mathcal{M} = \{1,\dots,K\} with generator Q = (q_{ij}). The hybrid SDE is

d\mathbf{X}(t) = \mathbf{f}_{\theta(t)}(\mathbf{X}(t)) \, dt + \boldsymbol{\sigma}_{\theta(t)}(\mathbf{X}(t)) \, d\mathbf{W}(t).

The infinitesimal generator for a test function V(\mathbf{x}, k) is

\mathcal{L}V(\mathbf{x}, k) = \nabla V \cdot \mathbf{f}_k + \frac12 \operatorname{tr}\!\big( \boldsymbol{\sigma}_k^{\!\top} \operatorname{Hess} V \, \boldsymbol{\sigma}_k \big) + \sum_{\ell \neq k} q_{k\ell} \big( V(\mathbf{x}, \ell) - V(\mathbf{x}, k) \big).

A.7.1 Large Deviations for Hybrid Diffusions

For a fixed regime k, the Freidlin–Wentzell action functional on [0,T] is

S_T(\varphi) = \frac12 \int_0^T \big\| \boldsymbol{\sigma}_k^{-1}(\varphi_t) (\dot{\varphi}_t - \mathbf{f}_k(\varphi_t)) \big\|^2 dt.

The quasipotential from an attractor \mathbf{x}^* to a point \mathbf{y} is

V_k(\mathbf{y}) = \inf_{T>0, \, \varphi(0)=\mathbf{x}^*, \, \varphi(T)=\mathbf{y}} S_T(\varphi).

When regime switching occurs, the joint quasipotential for a hybrid trajectory (\boldsymbol{\varphi}_t, \theta_t) with switching times t_m is (Budhiraja & Dupuis 2000)

V(\mathbf{z}, k) = \inf_{T, \boldsymbol{\varphi}, \theta} \left\{ \frac12 \int_0^T \big\| \boldsymbol{\sigma}_{\theta(t)}^{-1}(\dot{\boldsymbol{\varphi}} - \mathbf{f}_{\theta}) \big\|^2 dt + \sum_{m} \ln\!\left( \frac{q_{\theta(t_m^-)\,\theta(t_m^+)}}{-q_{\theta(t_m^-)\,\theta(t_m^-)}} \right) \right\}.

The probability of exit from a domain G within time T satisfies, for small noise intensity \epsilon,

\mathbb{P}(\tau_\epsilon \le T) \asymp \exp\!\big( - \frac{1}{\epsilon} \inf_{\partial G} V \big).

A.8 Homotopy Continuation for Boundary Equilibria

For polynomial or rational rate laws, the steady‑state equations \dot{\mathbf{z}}=0 form a square polynomial system. Homotopy continuation (e.g., via HomotopyContinuation.jl) can numerically find all isolated solutions, thereby enumerating every boundary equilibrium E_{\mathcal{J}}. This provides the input for the persistence checks in the MINLP lazy‑cut loop.

Appendix B — Proof Strategy for Conjecture 1 (Stoichiometric–Thermodynamic Feasibility)

Conjecture 1 (restated).
Assume the regularised kinetics (A.1) with finite p, a fixed processor configuration \mathbf{y}, and that the closed subsystem (\mathbf{b}=0) is weakly reversible with \delta = 0. Suppose the open‑system Jacobian \mathbf{J}_{\text{open}}(\epsilon) = \mathbf{N} \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}}{\partial (\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{r})} is nonsingular along the homotopy path \mathbf{N}\mathbf{f} = \epsilon\mathbf{b},\; \epsilon\in[0,1]. Then a strictly positive steady state exists iff \mathbf{b} \in \operatorname{cone}(\mathbf{N}) and \mathcal{F} \cap \mathcal{T}^{-1} \neq \emptyset (where \mathcal{T}^{-1} is the pre‑image of \mathcal{T} under the log‑resource mapping). Moreover, when it exists, the steady state is unique in its stoichiometric compatibility class and locally asymptotically stable.

B.1 Necessity

Trivial: any positive steady state yields fluxes \mathbf{f}^* \ge 0 with \mathbf{N}\mathbf{f}^* = \mathbf{b}, so \mathbf{b} \in \operatorname{cone}(\mathbf{N}), and the second law forces \mathbf{u}^* \in \mathcal{T}.

B.2 Sufficiency — Homotopy from the Closed Network

Consider the \epsilon-parametrised steady‑state equation

\mathbf{N} \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{z}) = \epsilon \mathbf{b}, \qquad \epsilon \in [0,1].
\tag{B.1}

At \epsilon = 0, the closed system has a unique positive equilibrium \mathbf{z}_0^* in its compatibility class, by the Deficiency Zero Theorem. The Jacobian \mathbf{J}_{\text{open}}(0) is exactly the Jacobian of the closed system, which is Hurwitz (asymptotic stability). By the Implicit Function Theorem, there exists \epsilon_0 > 0 and a smooth branch \mathbf{z}^*(\epsilon) of strictly positive equilibria for \epsilon \in [0, \epsilon_0).

The key task is to show that this branch can be extended to \epsilon = 1. The nonsingularity hypothesis on \mathbf{J}_{\text{open}}(\epsilon) along the whole path \epsilon \in [0,1] guarantees that no bifurcation is encountered, i.e., the Implicit Function Theorem can be applied iteratively to reach \epsilon = 1. This yields a positive steady state \mathbf{z}^*(1) for the full demand \mathbf{b}.

In practice, one may construct the path numerically using pseudo‑arclength continuation, verifying the nonsingularity condition a posteriori. The proof does not require the network to remain weakly reversible when \mathbf{b} \neq 0; the closed network only serves to provide a starting point.

B.3 Fixed‑Point Argument for Arbitrary \mathbf{b}

If the homotopy cannot be extended globally (e.g., a fold bifurcation occurs), we resort to a direct fixed‑point argument.

Define the set of log‑resources that can be realised by some flux \mathbf{f} \in \mathcal{F} and a compatible biomass \mathbf{x}:

\mathcal{U} = \{ \mathbf{u} \in \mathcal{T} \mid \exists \mathbf{f} \in \mathcal{F}, \; \mathbf{x} = (\operatorname{diag} \boldsymbol{\mu}^{(p)}(e^{\mathbf{u}}))^{-1} \mathbf{f}_{\text{guilds}}, \; \mathbf{f} = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}, e^{\mathbf{u}}) \}.

By strict monotonicity of \mu^{(p)}, the mapping from \mathbf{u} to \mathbf{x} is continuous and invertible. The constraints \mathbf{f} \in \mathcal{F} and \mathbf{u} \in \mathcal{T} define a closed (in the log‑space) and bounded set because resource concentrations cannot exceed the total mass in the system (dissipativity) and thermodynamic constraints impose upper bounds on certain log‑concentrations. Under mild assumptions, \mathcal{U} is a compact convex subset of \mathbb{R}^m (convexity follows from the linearity of the flux cone and the monotone properties of the harmonic mean).

Define a map \Phi : \mathcal{U} \to \mathcal{U} as follows:

1. Given \mathbf{u}, compute \mathbf{r} = e^{\mathbf{u}} and the corresponding biomass \mathbf{x} via \mathbf{x} = (\operatorname{diag} \boldsymbol{\mu}^{(p)}(\mathbf{r}))^{-1} \mathbf{f}_{\text{guilds}} where \mathbf{f} is a selected flux vector from \mathcal{F} (for uniqueness, one may take the minimum‑norm solution or use the flux that also satisfies the thermodynamic constraints).
2. With (\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{r}) determined, evaluate the resource consumption rates from \mathbf{N}_{\text{r}} \mathbf{f} and compute the steady‑state resource balance (including recycling). This yields an updated log‑resource vector \mathbf{u}' that satisfies the resource differential equations at equilibrium, while keeping the biomass equations fixed.

Because the system is closed and dissipative, \Phi maps \mathcal{U} into itself and is continuous. By Brouwer’s fixed‑point theorem, there exists \mathbf{u}^* \in \mathcal{U} with \Phi(\mathbf{u}^*) = \mathbf{u}^*, corresponding to a strictly positive steady state.

Rigorous verification of compactness and continuity requires detailed estimates on the growth functions and the resource recycling terms; this will be carried out in the thesis.

B.4 Uniqueness and Stability

Uniqueness in the stoichiometric compatibility class follows from the monotonicity of the rate laws and the fact that the closed network has a unique equilibrium. For the open system, if two positive steady states existed, their difference would lie in \operatorname{Im}\mathbf{N}, contradicting the uniqueness in the closed limit after a suitable scaling.

Local asymptotic stability is inherited from the closed system by continuity of the Jacobian eigenvalues. The extra external flux \mathbf{b} acts as a constant forcing that shifts but does not destabilise the equilibrium under the nonsingularity assumption.

Appendix C — Proof Strategy for Conjecture 2 (Deterministic Persistence)

Conjecture 2 (restated).
Under the conditions of Conjecture 1, the closed ecosystem with regularised kinetics is uniformly persistent iff at every boundary equilibrium E_{\mathcal{J}} there exists a missing guild i \notin \mathcal{J} with invasion growth rate \lambda_i(E_{\mathcal{J}}) > 0.

C.1 Carrying Simplex and Competitive Structure

For finite p, the vector field (5) is \mathcal{C}^\infty and competitive. Indeed, for j \neq i,

\frac{\partial \dot{x}_i}{\partial x_j} = 0,

because the per‑capita growth rate \mu_i^{(p)}(\mathbf{r}) - D_i depends only on \mathbf{r}, and \dot{r}_k terms involve sums over guilds; the cross‑derivative with respect to x_j is non‑positive because resource consumption by guild j reduces r_k, which in turn reduces \mu_i^{(p)}. A standard transformation (Hirsch 1988) puts the system into a competitive Kolmogorov form, guaranteeing the existence of a carrying simplex \Sigma.

C.2 Invasion Condition and Average Lyapunov Function

Let V(\mathbf{x}) = -\sum_i p_i \ln x_i with positive weights p_i to be chosen. Then

\frac{\dot{V}}{V} = \sum_i p_i \big( \mu_i^{(p)}(\mathbf{r}) - D_i \big).
\tag{C.1}

Because all boundary limit sets of competitive systems are equilibria (Smith 1995), uniform persistence is equivalent to the existence of weights p_i > 0 such that

\sum_i p_i \big( \mu_i^{(p)}(\mathbf{r}^*(E_{\mathcal{J}})) - D_i \big) > 0
\tag{C.2}

for every boundary equilibrium E_{\mathcal{J}}. If (6) holds for each boundary equilibrium (i.e., every E_{\mathcal{J}} has at least one missing invader with positive growth rate), we can set p_i large for those particular invaders and small for others, solving a linear programming feasibility problem. The solvability is guaranteed by the fact that the set of invasion growth rates forms a matrix with a positive column for each boundary equilibrium. A detailed constructive algorithm will be presented in the thesis.

C.3 Reduction to R^*-Rule

Using the regularised kinetics, the condition \mu_i^{(p)}(\mathbf{r}^*) > D_i is equivalent to

\Big( \sum_{j \in \mathcal{R}_i} \big( \mu_{ij}(r_j^*) \big)^{-p} \Big)^{-1/p} > D_i.

For large p, this approximates the exact minimum law, and the inequality reduces to r_j^* > R_{ij}^* for all limiting resources. The persistence condition therefore demands that for each boundary equilibrium, at least one missing guild sees all its critical resources above their break‑even thresholds. This is a multispecies generalisation of the chemostat R^* rule.

C.4 Uniformity Constant \kappa

Once uniform persistence is established, the compactness of the carrying simplex and the continuity of the invasion rates guarantee a uniform lower bound \kappa > 0 for all guilds, depending only on the system parameters.

Appendix D — Proof Strategy for Conjecture 2′ (Stochastic Permanence)

D.1 Lyapunov Function for Hybrid SDE

We use the same V(\mathbf{x}) = -\sum_i p_i \ln x_i. The generator in regime k is

\mathcal{L}V(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{r}, k) = -\sum_i p_i \big( \mu_i^{(p)}(\mathbf{r}) - D_i \big) + \frac12 \sum_i \frac{p_i}{x_i^2} [\boldsymbol{\sigma}_k \boldsymbol{\sigma}_k^{\!\top}]_{ii} + \text{cross terms involving resources}.

The drift part is strictly negative near the boundary because the deterministic invasion rates are positive. The diffusion term is problematic because of the 1/x_i^2 singularity. To control it, we assume that the noise coefficient \boldsymbol{\sigma}_k vanishes sufficiently fast as x_i \to 0; for instance, \boldsymbol{\sigma}_k(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{r}) = \operatorname{diag}(x_1, \dots, x_{n_s}) \cdot \tilde{\boldsymbol{\sigma}}_k(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{r}) (multiplicative noise). Then [\boldsymbol{\sigma}_k \boldsymbol{\sigma}_k^{\!\top}]_{ii} contains factors x_i^2, cancelling the singularity and leaving a bounded term. Under this structural assumption, \mathcal{L}V becomes strictly negative outside a compact interior set, guaranteeing positive recurrence by Has’minskii’s criterion.

D.2 Extinction Probability Bound

With multiplicative noise scaling like \sigma_0 \ll 1, the diffusion part is O(\sigma_0^2) while the drift near the boundary is negative of order O(1). The Freidlin–Wentzell theory for hybrid diffusions applies, and the quasipotential I_\varepsilon is the minimal action to reach the boundary. The exponential bound (8) follows directly from the large deviation principle, with C(T) polynomial in T. The explicit form of I_\varepsilon involves solving a variational problem that will be approximated numerically for the MVE safety constraints.

Appendix E — MINLP Formulation and Computational Procedures

E.1 Full MINLP Statement

The minimum viable ecosystem problem is:

\begin{aligned}
\min_{\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{y}, \mathbf{x}^*, \mathbf{f}^*, \mathbf{r}^*} \quad & \sum_{i=1}^{n_s} u_i + \sum_{j=1}^{n_Q} y_j \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & \mathbf{N}(\mathbf{y}) \mathbf{f}^* = \mathbf{b}, \\
& \mathbf{A} \ln \mathbf{r}^* < \mathbf{c}, \\
& \mathbf{x}_i^* \mu_i^{(p)}(\mathbf{r}^*) = f_i^* \quad \forall i \in S, \\
& \text{persistence cuts: for all candidate boundary equilibria } E_{\mathcal{J}}, \; \exists i \notin \mathcal{J} : \mu_i^{(p)}(\mathbf{r}^*(E_{\mathcal{J}})) > D_i, \\
& \mathbf{x}^* > 0, \; \mathbf{r}^* > 0, \; \mathbf{f}^* \ge 0, \\
& u_i \in \{0,1\},\; y_j \in \{0,1\}.
\end{aligned}

E.2 Handling Bilinear Terms

The term \mathbf{N}(\mathbf{y})\mathbf{f}^* couples binaries and continuous fluxes. We introduce auxiliary variables z_{kj} = y_j f_k^* and enforce

0 \le z_{kj} \le M y_j, \qquad f_k^* - M(1-y_j) \le z_{kj} \le f_k^*,

with M large enough. To tighten the relaxation, we use outer approximation and spatial branching.

The biomass equation is x_i \mu_i^{(p)}(\mathbf{r}) - f_i = 0. This can be handled by convex underestimators of \mu_i^{(p)} (which is concave in \mathbf{r} for the Monod function) or by piecewise linearisation.

E.3 Persistence Cut Generation

For a candidate integer solution (\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{y}), the steady‑state equations of the corresponding deterministic subsystem are solved numerically. All boundary equilibria E_{\mathcal{J}} are found using homotopy continuation for the reduced system where guilds with u_i=0 are set to zero. For each E_{\mathcal{J}}, the maximum over missing guilds of \mu_i^{(p)} - D_i is evaluated. If any E_{\mathcal{J}} fails the invasion test, the cut

\sum_{i \notin \mathcal{J}} u_i \ge 1

is added to the MINLP, and the solver resumes. The lazy‑cut loop is implemented via a callback in Juniper.jl.

E.4 Overall Algorithm

1. Pre‑processing: Build catalogue, check \mathbf{b} \in \operatorname{cone}(\mathbf{N}) via linear programming.
2. Branch‑and‑bound: Solve MINLP relaxation with Juniper/Ipopt.
3. Integer callback: When an integer node is found, run persistence checker.
   · If persistent, compare with current incumbent.
   · If not persistent, add cut, reject node.
4. Terminate when optimality gap is closed; return minimal component configuration.

This algorithm yields the MVE configuration that is stoichiometrically feasible, thermodynamically consistent, and deterministically persistent. Stochastic safety margins can be added by enforcing I_\varepsilon \ge -\sigma_0^2 \ln \delta in a post‑processing step.

